 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on with Pompe disease",
   "text": "Pompe disease happens when there are changes in a gene called GAA [Eb8a5930, Ea30102f, Ec4ca19d, Ebae57f3, E19e7fce]. GAA normally tells the body how to make an enzyme, which is a helper that speeds up chemical jobs in cells, and this one breaks down a stored sugar called glycogen inside small parts of the cell called lysosomes [Ec4ca19d, Ed4143fa, E5b4cd33]. When this enzyme is missing or does not work well, glycogen builds up inside the lysosomes, especially in muscle [Ea482757, Ec9fc8f0, E12d073c]. This is described as a problem with the lysosome itself, and also with the cell's clean-up process called autophagy, which normally helps clear waste out of cells [Efa6247d, E1b87a08].",
   "citations": [
    "Eb8a5930",
    "Ea30102f",
    "Ec4ca19d",
    "Ebae57f3",
    "E19e7fce",
    "Ed4143fa",
    "E5b4cd33",
    "Ea482757",
    "Ec9fc8f0",
    "E12d073c",
    "Efa6247d",
    "E1b87a08"
   ],
   "status": "ok"
  },
  {
   "key": "similar",
   "title": "How Pompe disease compares to other conditions",
   "text": "The GAA gene sits inside a group of related pathways that Reactome calls 'Glycogen storage diseases,' which brings together conditions that involve similar glycogen-handling biology [Eac0da0e, Eab2549c].",
   "citations": [
    "Eac0da0e",
    "Eab2549c"
   ],
   "status": "ok"
  },
  {
   "key": "what_exists",
   "title": "What research and support already exist",
   "text": "The Acid Maltase Deficiency Association, a patient group based in the United States, works on Pompe disease, according to their website [E516c908]. This group also runs a Patient Registry for Pompe disease, according to their website [E3fa57b9, Ec799c2f]. Pompe Deutschland e.V., a patient group based in Germany, also works on Pompe disease, according to their website [E1d8cf27, E696335b]. There is a study called the ZIP Study testing medicines called Cipaglucosidase Alfa and Miglustat in children with Pompe disease, run by Amicus Therapeutics, with sites in Australia, Canada, Germany, Italy, Japan, and the United States, according to ClinicalTrials.gov [Edbac865, E4d943dc, Ee74d13f, Ed5cbc50]. Another study is looking at antibody levels and biomarkers in blood in people with late-onset Pompe disease, run by Astellas Gene Therapies, with sites in several countries including the United States, Australia, Brazil, Canada, and France, according to ClinicalTrials.gov [E7687445, Ec9cdaf8, E3f6f261, E0c7b3d4, E39d5c99, Eb9a04d8]. In France, a study lets people keep receiving a medicine called Avalglucosidase alfa after an earlier trial ended, run by Genzyme, a Sanofi Company, according to ClinicalTrials.gov [E7147ec2, E2e90d53, Ec435dd0, E8592216]. In Taiwan, a study called the Pompe Disease RWE Long-term Monitoring Program is recruiting, run by National Taiwan University Hospital, according to ClinicalTrials.gov [E05add6f, Ee77aa3e, Eb143273]. In the United States, Duke University runs a study on managing late-onset Pompe disease, according to ClinicalTrials.gov [E30ce9a7, E1929246, E5b01652]. The NIH NICHD funds grants connected to Pompe disease, including one on newborn screening and one exploring a possible oligonucleotide treatment, according to NIH RePORTER [E67f497e, E523a0d8, E3fd67d6, E2b72142]. Researchers Daniel Woo and Kabir Jalal lead one of these funded projects, according to NIH RePORTER [Ee9acdad, E11b06fa].",
